            "primaryConceptId": "concept.ostomy-enterocutaneous-fistula.loperamide-cardiac-toxicity-management.v2",
            "currentUpdate": "Outside hospital record. The medication record documented a recent increase in prescribed loperamide for {patientName}'s jejunostomy losses. After palpitations and seated syncope, she received monitored assessment and saline. Creatinine, potassium and magnesium returned to their usual ranges. Repeat ECG showed a QTc of 570 ms compared with 430 ms before escalation. Her other medications had no identified QT-prolonging agent. Fluid replacement and cardiac monitoring were underway.",
            "stem": "Which medication plan should {patientName}'s outside team choose while cardiac monitoring and fluid replacement continue?",
            "answerChoices": [
              {
                "id": "choice.topic.ostomy-enterocutaneous-fistula.r3-loperamide-toxicity.a.v1",
                "label": "Withhold the current loperamide regimen",
                "isCorrect": true,
                "rationale": "Syncope and persistent marked QTc prolongation raise concern for toxicity after metabolic correction. FDA guidance supports prompt withdrawal for suspected toxicity.",
                "serviceRequest": null
              },
              {
                "id": "choice.topic.ostomy-enterocutaneous-fistula.r3-loperamide-toxicity.b.v1",
                "label": "Halve the current loperamide regimen",
                "isCorrect": false,
                "rationale": "A partial reduction preserves intestinal benefit. Suspected cardiac toxicity calls for prompt withdrawal during monitored treatment.",
                "serviceRequest": null
              },
              {
                "id": "choice.topic.ostomy-enterocutaneous-fistula.r3-loperamide-toxicity.c.v1",
                "label": "Continue the current loperamide regimen",
                "isCorrect": false,
                "rationale": "Specialist high-dose treatment can continue with appropriate surveillance. The symptomatic ECG course supplies a new reason to suspect toxicity.",
                "serviceRequest": null
              }
            ],
            "shuffleAnswers": true,
            "explanation": "The symptom pattern and persistent ECG change after metabolic recovery support suspected loperamide cardiac toxicity. Prompt withdrawal accompanies monitored treatment and fluid support. This instruction addresses the new toxicity signal during the recorded assessment.",
